== an unset name near a set name names it:
-c:1:24: warning: The variable 'output_fil' is never assigned, and 'output_file' is assigned. (SC2153)
     1 |  output_file=out; echo "$output_fil"
       |                         ^~~~~~~~~~~
-c:1:1: details:
     1 |  output_file=out; echo "$output_fil"
       |  ^~~~~~~~~~~ The value is assigned here.
note: Correct the spelling or assign 'output_fil'.
-c:1:24: warning: The variable 'output_fil' is not set and expands to an empty string.
     1 |  output_file=out; echo "$output_fil"
       |                         ^~~~~~~~~~~
note: The variable 'output_file' is set. Correct the spelling, or write ${output_fil-} if an empty expansion is intended.

== a transposed pair names the set name:
-c:1:30: warning: The variable 'destinaton_path' is never assigned, and 'destination_path' is assigned. (SC2153)
     1 |  destination_path=/tmp; echo "$destinaton_path"
       |                               ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
-c:1:1: details:
     1 |  destination_path=/tmp; echo "$destinaton_path"
       |  ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ The value is assigned here.
note: Correct the spelling or assign 'destinaton_path'.
-c:1:30: warning: The variable 'destinaton_path' is not set and expands to an empty string.
     1 |  destination_path=/tmp; echo "$destinaton_path"
       |                               ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: The variable 'destination_path' is set. Correct the spelling, or write ${destinaton_path-} if an empty expansion is intended.

== a name far from every set name keeps the plain note:
-c:1:30: warning: The variable 'zzzzzzzzzzzzzz' is used before any assignment gives it a value. (SC2154)
     1 |  destination_path=/tmp; echo "$zzzzzzzzzzzzzz"
       |                               ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Assign 'zzzzzzzzzzzzzz' before this use, or set it in the environment.
-c:1:30: warning: The variable 'zzzzzzzzzzzzzz' is not set and expands to an empty string.
     1 |  destination_path=/tmp; echo "$zzzzzzzzzzzzzz"
       |                               ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Replace it with ${zzzzzzzzzzzzzz-} if empty expansion is desired.

== nounset names the set name in its fatal note:
-c:1:40: error: The variable 'configuration_rot' is never assigned, and 'configuration_root' is assigned. (SC2153)
     1 |  set -u; configuration_root=/etc; echo "$configuration_rot"
       |                                         ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
-c:1:9: details:
     1 |  set -u; configuration_root=/etc; echo "$configuration_rot"
       |          ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ The value is assigned here.
note: Correct the spelling or assign 'configuration_rot'.
rc=1
== an exported name is a candidate:
-c:1:7: warning: The variable 'DEMO_TEST_EXPORTE' is used before any assignment gives it a value. (SC2154)
     1 |  echo "$DEMO_TEST_EXPORTE"
       |        ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Assign 'DEMO_TEST_EXPORTE' before this use, or set it in the environment.
-c:1:7: warning: The variable 'DEMO_TEST_EXPORTE' is not set and expands to an empty string.
     1 |  echo "$DEMO_TEST_EXPORTE"
       |        ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: The variable 'DEMO_TEST_EXPORTED' is set. Correct the spelling, or write ${DEMO_TEST_EXPORTE-} if an empty expansion is intended.

